rows=[]
def q(*a,**k):rows.append(question(*a,**k))

q('Loud snoring, witnessed pauses and excessive daytime sleepiness occur in a patient with obesity. What is the best diagnostic principle?', 'Evaluate for OSA with an appropriate objective sleep test rather than diagnosing from symptoms or a questionnaire alone',[
 ('A high STOP-BANG score alone establishes severity','It identifies risk, not the definitive physiological diagnosis.'),('Absence of recalled awakenings excludes OSA','Patients may not remember events.'),('Normal daytime oxygen saturation excludes OSA','The obstruction is sleep-related.')],
 'Screening and diagnostic testing have different roles', '**OSA involves recurrent sleep-related upper-airway obstruction.**', ['Symptoms and comorbidities guide pretest probability.','PSG or suitable home testing quantifies respiratory events.'], '**Choose the test for the patient.**', ['Comorbidity and suspected hypoventilation affect selection.','Assess driving risk from sleepiness.'], [['Questionnaire','Risk screening'],['Sleep test','Objective diagnosis'],['Daytime saturation','Not an exclusion']], [OS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with neuromuscular weakness and suspected nocturnal hypoventilation needs sleep assessment. Which test is preferred over routine home apnea testing?', 'Attended polysomnography with appropriate physiological monitoring',[
 ('A questionnaire alone','It does not measure ventilation.'),('A negative home test always excludes all sleep-disordered breathing','Home tests have limitations in complex disease.'),('No test if daytime saturation is normal','Nocturnal hypoventilation can precede daytime abnormalities.')],
 'A complex ventilatory problem needs more than obstruction screening', '**AASM recommends PSG in potential respiratory muscle weakness or suspected hypoventilation.**', ['Significant cardiorespiratory disease and chronic opioids are other relevant contexts.','The required monitoring depends on the suspected disorder.'], '**Interpret test limitations explicitly.**', ['Do not substitute an uncomplicated-OSA pathway.','Coordinate respiratory support if indicated.'], [['Uncomplicated OSA risk','Home test may suit'],['Hypoventilation/weakness','PSG preferred'],['Goal','Characterize mechanism']], [OS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A technically adequate home apnea test is negative, but clinical suspicion for OSA remains high. What is the next recommended step?', 'Polysomnography',[
 ('Permanently exclude OSA','A home test can miss disease.'),('Repeat questionnaires instead of objective testing indefinitely','This does not resolve the physiological question.'),('Diagnose severe OSA solely from snoring','Symptoms alone do not establish severity.')],
 'A negative limited test does not end a high-probability assessment', '**AASM recommends PSG after a negative, inconclusive or inadequate home test.**', ['Home testing can underestimate events because of measurement limitations.','Review the original recording and clinical context.'], '**Reassess alternative sleepiness causes too.**', ['Insufficient sleep, medicines and other disorders may coexist.','Do not let one diagnosis obscure another.'], [['Home test negative','Consider false negative'],['High suspicion','Proceed to PSG'],['Sleepiness','Assess other contributors']], [OS])
q('What is the main therapeutic action of CPAP in OSA?', 'Pneumatic splinting of the upper airway during sleep',[
 ('Direct regeneration of pharyngeal nerves','This is not the mechanism.'),('Suppression of every REM cycle','That is not the treatment target.'),('Permanent cure after one night regardless of adherence','Benefit usually requires continued effective use.')],
 'Pressure prevents recurrent collapse', '**PAP reduces obstructive events when effectively delivered.**', ['It can improve sleepiness and sleep-related quality of life.','Interface fit, leak and adherence influence benefit.'], '**Follow treatment rather than issuing a device only.**', ['Troubleshoot comfort and residual events.','Weight, positional and other selected interventions may complement care.'], [['Mechanism','Upper-airway splint'],['Outcome','Fewer obstruction events'],['Follow-up','Adherence and effectiveness']], [PAP])
q('What defines obesity hypoventilation syndrome?', 'Obesity with sleep-disordered breathing and awake PaCO2 at least 45 mmHg after excluding other causes of hypoventilation',[
 ('Obesity and snoring alone','Awake hypercapnia and exclusions are required.'),('Any nocturnal desaturation regardless of body habitus','This is not the definition.'),('A normal daytime bicarbonate always proves severe OHS','Bicarbonate is a screening aid, not that conclusion.')],
 'OHS includes daytime ventilatory failure', '**The ATS definition includes BMI at least 30 kg/m² and awake hypercapnia.**', ['Other pulmonary, neurological and metabolic causes need assessment.','Severe OSA often coexists.'], '**Select PAP and weight treatment appropriately.**', ['Stable patients with severe OSA may begin CPAP; other contexts can require NIV.','Acute respiratory failure needs a separate urgent pathway.'], [['Obesity','BMI at least 30'],['Awake PaCO2','At least 45 mmHg'],['Other causes','Exclude']], [OH])
q('What is recommended first-line behavioral treatment for chronic insomnia disorder?', 'Multicomponent cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia',[
 ('Sleep hygiene advice alone as an equivalent complete treatment','AASM does not recommend sleep hygiene as sole treatment.'),('Long-term sedative escalation for every patient','Risk and indication require assessment.'),('Avoid a regular wake time to compensate indefinitely','Irregular schedules can perpetuate the problem.')],
 'CBT-I addresses perpetuating behaviors and beliefs', '**It combines components such as stimulus control, sleep scheduling and cognitive work.**', ['Education supports rather than replaces active therapy.','Adapt treatment to comorbidities and safety.'], '**Assess the cause and consequences of insomnia.**', ['Review mood, pain, medications and sleep apnea.','Use a sleep diary and functional outcomes.'], [['CBT-I','Multicomponent treatment'],['Sleep hygiene','Supportive, not sole therapy'],['Assessment','Comorbidity and daytime impact']], [IN])
q('What is the aim of stimulus control in CBT-I?', 'Rebuild the association between bed and sleep rather than prolonged wakefulness and frustration',[
 ('Spend progressively more waking hours in bed','This can strengthen the undesired association.'),('Use alcohol as a nightly conditioning aid','This can fragment sleep and cause other harm.'),('Eliminate every normal brief awakening','That is not a realistic treatment goal.')],
 'Learned sleep associations are modifiable', '**Consistent wake timing and appropriate bed use are key elements.**', ['The plan is adapted to mobility and safety.','Sleep restriction/compression is a related but distinct component.'], '**Measure improvement over time.**', ['A diary helps guide changes.','Address unrealistic expectations and sleep-related worry.'], [['Bed','Cue for sleep'],['Wake time','Consistency'],['CBT-I','Several complementary components']], [IN])
q('Uncomfortable leg sensations create an urge to move that starts at rest, improves with movement and is worse in the evening. Which diagnosis is favored?', 'Restless legs syndrome',[
 ('Nocturnal calf cramp as the identical syndrome','Cramps are painful focal contractions rather than the characteristic urge pattern.'),('Peripheral neuropathy alone in every case','Neuropathy can mimic or coexist but does not always show rest/movement/circadian features.'),('Parkinsonian rest tremor','This is a movement oscillation rather than a sensory urge.')],
 'Four characteristic features need mimic exclusion', '**RLS is clinical: urge, rest provocation, movement relief and evening/night predominance.**', ['Exclude positional discomfort, cramps and other mimics.','Assess iron status and aggravating medicines.'], '**Treat underlying contributors.**', ['Untreated OSA, caffeine and selected drugs can worsen symptoms.','Choose sustainable therapy rather than reflex dopamine escalation.'], [['Rest','Worse'],['Movement','Temporary relief'],['Evening','Circadian predominance']], [RL], 'clinical_scenario')
q('What laboratory assessment is especially important in clinically significant RLS?', 'Ferritin and transferrin saturation, interpreted using RLS-specific iron guidance',[
 ('Hemoglobin alone excludes relevant iron deficiency','Iron status can be relevant without anemia.'),('A high normal-range ferritin always excludes low available iron','Inflammation and transferrin saturation affect interpretation.'),('No laboratory work is useful','Iron evaluation can directly change treatment.')],
 'Brain-iron considerations differ from simple anemia screening', '**AASM recommends regular appropriate iron studies.**', ['Consensus thresholds include ferritin at or below 75 ng/mL or transferrin saturation below 20% for considering replacement.','Route depends on the iron profile and clinical context.'], '**Avoid unsupervised indiscriminate supplementation.**', ['Assess overload risk and monitor response.','Do not apply a generic laboratory normal range without clinical interpretation.'], [['Ferritin','Storage context'],['Transferrin saturation','Available iron context'],['Treatment','Profile- and guideline-based']], [RL])
q('A patient on long-term pramipexole for RLS develops symptoms earlier in the day, with spread to the arms and increasing dose needs. What complication is suggested?', 'Dopaminergic augmentation',[
 ('Proof that doubling the dopamine agonist is always the best solution','Escalation can perpetuate augmentation.'),('A normal inevitable progression requiring no review','The pattern requires treatment reassessment.'),('An isolated acute stroke as the typical explanation','The medication-linked evolving sensory syndrome favors augmentation.')],
 'Treatment can worsen the timing and distribution of the syndrome', '**Augmentation is a major long-term dopamine-therapy concern in RLS.**', ['Earlier onset and spread are characteristic clues.','Distinguish it from simple loss of effect or an untreated trigger.'], '**Plan a supervised transition.**', ['Assess iron and non-dopaminergic options.','Avoid abrupt withdrawal that can cause severe rebound.'], [['Onset','Earlier in day'],['Distribution','Can spread'],['Action','Specialist regimen review']], [RL], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which drug approach is favored in the 2025 AASM guideline for suitable adults needing chronic RLS medication?', 'An alpha-2-delta ligand such as gabapentin, gabapentin enacarbil or pregabalin',[
 ('Routine dopamine agonist escalation as the universal first choice','Augmentation changes the long-term risk-benefit balance.'),('A high-dose antipsychotic as routine treatment','Dopamine blockade can aggravate RLS.'),('No iron review before medication','Iron status remains central.')],
 'The guideline emphasizes sustainable long-term control', '**Gabapentinoids have strong recommendations in appropriate adult RLS.**', ['Sedation, falls, respiratory risk and renal function affect selection.','Iron treatment is indicated in suitable iron profiles.'], '**Individualize rather than declaring one drug safe for everyone.**', ['Some refractory cases need specialist alternatives.','Review aggravating drugs and untreated sleep apnea.'], [['Preferred class','Alpha-2-delta ligands'],['Iron','Assess and treat when indicated'],['Dopamine agonists','Routine use discouraged']], [RL])
q('Does periodic leg movement on a sleep study automatically establish periodic limb movement disorder?', 'No; clinically important impairment and exclusion of relevant alternative causes are required',[
 ('Yes, any isolated movement is a disorder','Incidental movements are common.'),('Yes, regardless of untreated OSA or RLS','These can explain the movements or symptoms.'),('No, therefore movements can never matter','A properly defined clinical disorder can be relevant.')],
 'A recorded event and a clinical disorder are different', '**PLMD requires a qualifying movement burden with associated symptoms not better explained otherwise.**', ['RLS and untreated sleep-disordered breathing complicate attribution.','A study number alone is insufficient.'], '**Treat the actual clinical problem.**', ['Review sleep quality, medicines and comorbidities.','Do not reflexively medicate an incidental PSG finding.'], [['PLMS','Recorded movements'],['PLMD','Clinical diagnosis'],['Attribution','Exclude better explanations']], [RL])
q('A patient punches and kicks while acting out vivid dreams; PSG demonstrates REM sleep without atonia. Which diagnosis is supported?', 'REM sleep behavior disorder',[
 ('Simple primary insomnia','This does not explain REM motor disinhibition.'),('Every dream enactment episode proves RBD without PSG assessment','OSA, seizures and other parasomnias can mimic it.'),('A deliberate behavior disorder as the required explanation','The motor behavior can arise during sleep.')],
 'Dream enactment requires physiological confirmation and context', '**RBD combines a compatible history with REM motor-tone abnormality.**', ['Medication-associated and neurological contexts need review.','Bed-partner history can be especially useful.'], '**Prioritize injury prevention.**', ['Remove dangerous objects and adapt the sleeping environment.','Address coexisting apnea and other mimics.'], [['History','Dream enactment'],['PSG','REM without atonia'],['Immediate care','Injury prevention']], [RB], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which management principle is central to RBD?', 'A safer sleep environment, with individualized medication such as immediate-release melatonin or clonazepam when appropriate',[
 ('Medication makes environmental precautions unnecessary','Injury risk can persist.'),('Clonazepam is risk-free in older adults with OSA and falls','Sedation, balance and breathing risks matter.'),('Every isolated RBD case will develop the same disease at an exact date','Neurodegenerative risk is increased but individual prediction is uncertain.')],
 'Reduce injury while treating symptoms', '**AASM makes conditional medication recommendations rather than a universal drug mandate.**', ['Choice depends on age, cognition, falls and breathing.','Review inciting medicines in drug-associated cases.'], '**Discuss neurological follow-up sensitively.**', ['Isolated RBD can precede synucleinopathies.','Risk counseling should reflect uncertainty and patient preferences.'], [['Environment','Always address safety'],['Medication','Individualized'],['Follow-up','Neurological and sleep review']], [RB])
q('Brief bilateral loss of muscle tone is triggered by laughter while consciousness remains intact in a sleepy patient. Which phenomenon is most characteristic?', 'Cataplexy',[
 ('Typical syncope','Consciousness is usually impaired in syncope.'),('An obligatory generalized convulsive seizure','The trigger and retained awareness favor another mechanism.'),('Myotonia','This is delayed relaxation rather than sudden loss of tone.')],
 'Emotion-triggered atonia is a diagnostic clue', '**Cataplexy strongly supports narcolepsy type 1 in the appropriate setting.**', ['It can be partial, affecting jaw or knees.','Other collapse disorders still need assessment.'], '**Evaluate the full hypersomnolence syndrome.**', ['Ask about sleep paralysis and dreamlike sleep-transition experiences.','Use objective testing through a sleep service.'], [['Trigger','Often emotion'],['Awareness','Preserved'],['Association','Narcolepsy type 1']], [NA], 'clinical_scenario')
q('Which MSLT pattern supports narcolepsy in the proper clinical and testing context?', 'Mean sleep latency at most eight minutes with at least two sleep-onset REM periods, applying the preceding-PSG rule appropriately',[
 ('Any single nap with dreaming','This is not sufficient.'),('A short latency after severe sleep deprivation as definitive proof','Sleep deprivation can confound the test.'),('A normal questionnaire as a substitute for the study','The diagnostic pathway needs appropriate objective evidence.')],
 'The physiological threshold needs valid preparation', '**The preceding PSG and clinical history are part of interpretation.**', ['A qualifying PSG SOREMP can count under the diagnostic rules.','Untreated OSA, insufficient sleep and medicines can distort results.'], '**Do not diagnose from the number alone.**', ['Use specialist review and exclusion of alternatives.','Orexin testing has a role in selected cases.'], [['Latency','At most eight minutes'],['SOREMPs','At least two with specified rules'],['Validity','Preparation and confounders']], [MS,NA])
q('What preparation is important before MSLT?', 'Document adequate sleep and a stable schedule, address relevant sleep disorders and review alerting/REM-modifying medicines through a safe plan',[
 ('Intentionally deprive the patient of sleep to improve sensitivity','This can produce misleading results.'),('Stop every medicine abruptly the night before','Withdrawal and REM rebound can confound testing and be unsafe.'),('Perform the test without a preceding sleep assessment','The overnight and longitudinal context matter.')],
 'An objective test can still be invalidated by preparation', '**AASM recommends sleep documentation, often for two weeks, and medication planning.**', ['Actigraphy can complement a diary.','Treat relevant OSA and account for shift work.'], '**Balance washout validity against safety.**', ['Use a clinician-supervised plan.','Record unavoidable confounders explicitly.'], [['Sleep schedule','Document adequacy'],['Medicines','Supervised review'],['Comorbidity','Optimize relevant disorders']], [MS])
q('Sleep onset is consistently very late, but sleep is normal in duration and quality when the person follows their preferred schedule. Which distinction is important?', 'A delayed circadian sleep-wake pattern rather than assuming an inability to sleep at any time',[
 ('Severe OSA is proven by late bedtime alone','Timing does not establish respiratory obstruction.'),('The person necessarily has no genuine impairment','Conflict with work or school can be disabling.'),('Long-term sedative escalation is the only possible intervention','Schedule and circadian strategies may be relevant.')],
 'Clock timing and sleep ability are different dimensions', '**A diary and actigraphy can reveal a stable delayed pattern.**', ['Exclude insufficient sleep, shift work and other explanations.','The preferred-schedule response is informative.'], '**Use a timing-aware treatment plan.**', ['Light and melatonin effects depend on timing and the diagnosis.','Seek sleep expertise rather than random bedtime dosing.'], [['Timing','Delayed'],['Preferred schedule','Sleep can be relatively normal'],['Assessment','Longitudinal diary/actigraphy']], [MS,IN], 'clinical_scenario')
q('An adult has chronic daytime sleepiness while routinely sleeping five hours because of work. What should be addressed before labeling a central hypersomnia?', 'Insufficient sleep and schedule-related sleep loss',[
 ('Narcolepsy is established from sleepiness alone','Sleepiness has many causes.'),('Stimulants remove the need to assess sleep duration','The underlying deficit remains.'),('A short MSLT after deprivation is uniquely diagnostic','Insufficient sleep can confound objective testing.')],
 'The most basic sleep exposure can be the cause', '**Adequate habitual sleep is necessary for valid hypersomnolence assessment.**', ['Review sedatives, mood and sleep-disordered breathing.','A diary can expose chronic restriction.'], '**Reassess after correcting the schedule where possible.**', ['Persistent symptoms may justify further testing.','Discuss safety, including drowsy driving.'], [['Sleep opportunity','Establish adequacy'],['Confounders','Drugs and other sleep disorders'],['Persistent sleepiness','Further evaluation']], [MS], 'clinical_scenario')
q('A sleepy patient has near misses while driving. What is the appropriate immediate principle?', 'Address driving and occupational safety promptly while evaluating and treating the cause',[
 ('Wait for every laboratory result before discussing safety','The immediate risk is already evident.'),('Caffeine guarantees safe driving','It is not a reliable solution to severe sleepiness.'),('A sleep diagnosis automatically determines every legal rule worldwide','Local licensing and occupational requirements vary.')],
 'Functional risk can precede a definitive diagnosis', '**Uncontrolled sleepiness can cause serious injury.**', ['Assess recent events and high-risk tasks.','Explain local safety responsibilities and practical alternatives.'], '**Treat and reassess objective and subjective function.**', ['OSA, insufficient sleep and central hypersomnias have different pathways.','Do not equate a prescription with restored safe performance.'], [['Immediate issue','Safety'],['Diagnosis','Find the cause'],['Follow-up','Demonstrate effective control']], [OS,MS])

